## 5. DAX STANDARDS
- Measures for aggregation. Calculated columns only for row-level attributes that must slice, filter or relate - and prefer building those in Power Query or the source.
- VAR ... RETURN for anything non-trivial. DIVIDE() for every division. No IFERROR or ISERROR for control flow.
- CALCULATE filters: column predicates ('Date'[Year] = 2026) instead of FILTER(ALL(table), ...). FILTER only over a column list or when the condition needs a measure. KEEPFILTERS to intersect instead of overwrite; REMOVEFILTERS or ALL to clear filters entirely.
- Share of total: when a measure must respect the current slicer/filter context but ignore ONLY the item's own filter (e.g. each client's share of the visible total), the denominator uses ALLSELECTED(<column>) - never REMOVEFILTERS, which clears the slicer context too and returns the grand total. Pattern: DIVIDE ( [Notional (ZAR)], CALCULATE ( [Notional (ZAR)], ALLSELECTED ( 'Client'[Client Name] ) ) ).
- Context transition is deliberate: measure references and CALCULATE inside iterators trigger it. Avoid iterating a large fact with a measure call when a column expression works.
- Time intelligence needs a contiguous Date table marked as date table, related on a date column, with Auto date/time off. Use DATESYTD, TOTALYTD, SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR, DATEADD, PARALLELPERIOD, or calendar-based time intelligence where the model defines calendars. Fiscal year-end argument: DATESYTD('Date'[Date], "06-30").
- SELECTEDVALUE for single-selection logic; ISINSCOPE for hierarchy-level logic; HASONEVALUE only when the test itself is needed.
- Window functions OFFSET, INDEX, WINDOW, RANK, ROWNUMBER (with ORDERBY, PARTITIONBY, MATCHBY) for previous-row, ranking and running logic on non-date axes.
- Visual calculations (RUNNINGSUM, MOVINGAVERAGE, PREVIOUS, NEXT, FIRST, LAST, COLLAPSE, EXPAND) for visual-local maths that nothing else reuses.
- Calculation groups for families of variants (time intelligence, currency, scenario): SELECTEDMEASURE(), ISSELECTEDMEASURE(), SELECTEDMEASURENAME(), formatStringDefinition; set precedence when several groups combine.
- Field parameters for user-switchable measures or axes: a table constructor of ("Label", NAMEOF('Table'[Measure]), order).
- User-defined functions (GA from the June 2026 release; compatibility level 1702 or higher):
  FUNCTION ConvertAmount = ( amount : NUMERIC, rate : DOUBLE = 1 ) => amount * rate
  Parameter types AnyVal, Scalar, Table, AnyRef, CalendarRef, ColumnRef, MeasureRef, TableRef; subtypes Int64, Decimal, Double, String, DateTime, Boolean, Numeric, Variant; modes val (eager, default) or expr (lazy - required for references and for changing context inside the function), e.g. t : TABLE EXPR, m : MEASUREREF. Save from DAX query view (DEFINE FUNCTION ..., then Update model with changes) or TMDL view (createOrReplace with a function object). Stored in definition/functions.tmdl. No recursion, overloading, display folders or hiding. Names: letters, digits, underscores, dots for namespaces.
- Formatting belongs to the measure: formatString ("#,##0", "#,##0.00", "0.0%") or a dynamic format string (formatStringDefinition). Never FORMAT() for numbers shown in visuals - it returns text and breaks sorting, totals and conditional formatting.
- Return BLANK() rather than 0 unless zeros are required.
- Naming: Title Case with units where useful ("Notional (ZAR)"); reference measures without a table prefix; always table-qualify columns ('Trades'[Notional]).
- Performance: avoid FILTER over a fact table, nested iterators over facts, bi-directional filters and high-cardinality SUMMARIZE; prove fixes with DAX Studio Server Timings (storage engine vs formula engine, callbacks) and Performance Analyzer.

## 6. POWER QUERY (M) STANDARDS
- Always the complete query with meaningful step names (#"Filtered Trades", not #"Changed Type1").
- Protect query folding: source-side filters, column selection, renames, types, grouping and same-source joins first; non-foldable steps (Index, custom functions, Table.Buffer, cross-source merges) last. Check the step folding indicators or View Native Query.
- Remove unused columns and rows early. Set types once and explicitly: Int64.Type for keys, Currency.Type for money when 4 decimals suffice, and a culture argument for text-to-date or text-to-number conversion.
- Parameters for server, database, paths and environment - they drive deployment rules.
- Incremental refresh: DateTime parameters RangeStart and RangeEnd; filter [Col] >= RangeStart and [Col] < RangeEnd (one inclusive side only); the filter must fold.
- Native SQL: Value.NativeQuery(Source, sql, null, [EnableFolding = true]) instead of the Query option, where the connector supports it.
- Web APIs: Web.Contents(staticBaseUrl, [RelativePath = ..., Query = [...]]) so the Service can refresh; paginate with List.Generate; never put credentials in M.
- Errors: try ... otherwise with a reason; never silently drop error rows.
- Formula.Firewall errors: restructure (staging queries, combine inside one query) before touching privacy levels; never recommend ignoring privacy levels for confidential data.

## 7. SEMANTIC MODEL STANDARDS
- Star schema: narrow facts (keys, dates, numbers) and descriptive dimensions; flatten snowflakes in Power Query.
- One-to-many, single-direction relationships from dimension to fact. Bi-directional only with a stated reason (prefer CROSSFILTER inside a measure). Many-to-many through a bridge table.
- Role-playing dates: one active relationship plus inactive ones used through USERELATIONSHIP, or duplicate dimensions when both must sit on an axis together.
- Hide keys and raw fact columns that measures cover; summarizeBy none on keys, codes and years; Sort by column for month and day names; data categories for geography and URLs; descriptions on measures; display folders; one dedicated measures table.
- Storage modes: Import by default; DirectQuery for real-time or very large sources (then Apply buttons, fewer visuals, aggregations); Dual for dimensions shared in composite models; Direct Lake for Fabric Lakehouse or Warehouse Delta tables (know the DirectQuery fallback and guardrails).
- Size: cut cardinality (split datetime into date and time, round decimals, drop GUIDs and unused columns), integer keys, no calculated columns on large facts. Measure with DAX Studio VertiPaq Analyzer.
- Large facts: incremental refresh, hybrid tables, user-defined aggregations.
- Security: RLS roles with DAX table filters; dynamic RLS through USERPRINCIPALNAME() against a security table; test with "View as". RLS restricts Viewers only - workspace Admins, Members and Contributors bypass it. OLS through TMDL or Tabular Editor.

## 8. PBIP / PBIR ENGINEERING - PAGE AND VISUAL CONSTRUCTION
8.1 Project layout
<Name>.pbip
<Name>.SemanticModel/definition/   database.tmdl, model.tmdl, relationships.tmdl, expressions.tmdl, functions.tmdl, tables/<Table>.tmdl, roles/, cultures/
<Name>.SemanticModel/.pbi/          cache - never edit
<Name>.Report/definition.pbir       datasetReference byPath "../<Name>.SemanticModel"; version "4.0" or higher for PBIR
<Name>.Report/definition/           version.json, report.json, reportExtensions.json (report-level measures), bookmarks/
<Name>.Report/definition/pages/pages.json                            pageOrder, activePageName
<Name>.Report/definition/pages/<page>/page.json
<Name>.Report/definition/pages/<page>/visuals/<visual>/visual.json   (+ optional mobile.json)
<Name>.Report/StaticResources/      themes, images (RegisteredResources)

8.2 Hard rules
1. PBIR only. If <Name>.Report/report.json exists and definition/ does not, the report is PBIR-Legacy: never edit it. Tell the user to enable File > Options and settings > Options > Preview features > "Store reports using enhanced metadata format (PBIR)" if it is not already on, open the .pbip, Save, and choose Upgrade.
2. Build on a project that Power BI Desktop saved. Never generate definition.pbir, version.json, report.json or .platform from scratch.
3. Power BI Desktop must be closed while files change and reopened afterwards - it does not hot-reload external edits and overwrites them on save. The same applies to TMDL file edits.
4. Back up first (git commit, or the backup step in template 8.7). Keep backups outside the project folder.
5. Names: a new page or visual "name" is a fresh 20-character lowercase hex string (e.g. 3f9c2a7d1b8e4f60a5c1), unique in the report; its folder name equals it. Legal characters: letters, digits, underscore, hyphen. Existing names such as ReportSection1a2b3c stay as they are.
6. $schema: reuse the exact URL and version from an existing file of the same kind in the project; the templates below show shape only. page.json takes only the properties its schema allows - no visualContainers array (visuals are discovered from folders).
7. Every projection has field, queryRef ("<Table>.<Field>") and nativeQueryRef (display name). Entity = table name, Property = column or measure name, exact case and spaces, no quotes or brackets. If the same field appears twice in one visual, give each nativeQueryRef a unique suffix.
8. Field kinds: "Measure" for DAX measures, "Column" for columns, "Aggregation" for implicit aggregation of a column (Function 0 Sum, 1 Avg, 3 Min, 4 Max). Prefer creating a measure. Never bind a physical column as "Measure".
9. Placement: visual.query holds queryState and optional sortDefinition; drillFilterOtherVisuals sits under visual; filterConfig sits at the container root. Container formatting (title, subTitle, background, border, dropShadow, padding, visualHeader) goes in visual.visualContainerObjects; visual formatting (labels, legend, categoryAxis, valueAxis, dataPoint, header, items, data and so on) goes in visual.objects.
10. Literal values inside expr.Literal.Value: text "'Sales'", boolean "true", double "12D", integer "3L". Colour: {"solid":{"color":{"expr":{"Literal":{"Value":"'#118DFF'"}}}}}. Theme JSON uses plain values, not expr wrappers.
11. When a visual sets any visualContainerObjects entry, also declare padding (8D on all sides) - otherwise the theme padding cascade is dropped and content sits flush against the border.
12. Unknown visualType, role or formatting property: don't guess. Ask the user to add one such visual in Desktop, save, and paste its visual.json; clone its structure. If Microsoft's powerbi-report-author CLI is installed, validate with: powerbi-report-author validate "<path>\<Name>.Report"
13. New page = page folder + page.json + its name appended to pages.json pageOrder using read-modify-write, never a blind overwrite.
14. Bookmarks capture page state: create them in Desktop; only clone existing bookmark files together with their page and visuals.
15. PBIR files can store data values (filter and slicer selections). Treat them as confidential in Git.
16. After edits the user reopens the .pbip. A blocking error names the file: ask for the exact message and fix only that file. Non-blocking warnings (for example an invalid activePageName) are auto-fixed by Desktop.
17. To locate objects: in Options > Report settings enable "Copy object names when right clicking on report objects", then right-click a visual > Copy object name - that is its folder name.

8.3 Canvas and layout defaults
1280 x 720, displayOption "FitToPage". 8 px grid: x, y, width and height are multiples of 8. Margins 24, gutters 16. Title textbox at y 16 (height 48, or 80 when it shares the top band with slicers); slicer strip top-right at y 16 (height 80); KPI row at y 112 (cards 120 high, four across at x 24, 336, 648, 960, width 296); main visuals from y 248 down to y 696. z and tabOrder follow reading order in steps of 1000. At most 8 data visuals per page.

