Here-strings must be single-quoted (@' ... '@) so "$schema" is not expanded, and the closing '@ must start at column 1. To add visuals to an existing page, set $page to that page's folder name and leave out the page.json entry; the pages.json step then changes nothing.

## 9. REPORT DESIGN STANDARDS
- One question per page; the most important number top-left; trend or comparison in the middle; detail at the bottom or behind drillthrough.
- Chart choice: trend -> line; ranking or comparison -> bar (horizontal for long labels); part-to-whole with 5 or fewer parts -> stacked bar or donut; distribution -> column histogram; correlation -> scatter; variance and bridges -> waterfall; exact values -> table or matrix; single KPI -> card or KPI visual.
- Titles state the measure and grain, or the insight ("Notional by Month, YTD").
- Colour: theme dataColors only, one accent for emphasis; red/green only for signed variance and always with a second cue (sign or icon); text contrast 4.5:1 or better.
- Number formats come from measure format strings, not per-visual overrides.
- Accessibility: alt text on every data visual, logical tab order, nothing conveyed by colour alone.
- Interaction: set edit interactions deliberately; drillthrough for detail; tooltip pages (page type Tooltip, 320 x 240); bookmarks and buttons for view toggles; field parameters for user-chosen axes and measures; sync slicers across pages where the same filter applies.
- Themes: name, dataColors, background, foreground, tableAccent, textClasses (callout, title, header, label: fontFace, fontSize, color) and visualStyles ("*": {"*": {...}}) with plain values.
- Performance: few visuals, no high-cardinality tables on landing pages, Apply buttons on DirectQuery slicers, Performance Analyzer before and after.

## 10. SERVICE, ALM AND ADMIN
- Publishing: Desktop Publish; Fabric Git integration (PBIP in Azure DevOps or GitHub); deployment pipelines (Dev -> Test -> Prod with deployment rules and parameters); fabric-cicd (Python) or the Fabric REST item definition APIs (reports deployed that way need a byConnection datasetReference).
- REST from PowerShell: Install-Module MicrosoftPowerBIMgmt -Scope CurrentUser; Connect-PowerBIServiceAccount; Invoke-PowerBIRestMethod -Method Post -Url "groups/<workspaceId>/datasets/<datasetId>/refreshes" -Body '{"notifyOption":"NoNotification"}'. Poll with GET on the same URL plus ?$top=1 (escape $ inside PowerShell double-quoted strings). Enhanced refresh (objects, commitMode, maxParallelism) needs Premium, PPU or Fabric capacity.
- XMLA endpoint: powerbi://api.powerbi.com/v1.0/myorg/<Workspace Name> (Premium, PPU or Fabric capacity; read-write enabled on the capacity).
- Refresh limits: 8 scheduled refreshes a day on shared capacity (Pro), 48 on Premium, PPU or Fabric capacity.
- Licensing: Free, Pro, Premium Per User, Fabric capacity (F SKUs; F64 and above let free-licence users view content). Premium P SKUs are being retired in favour of F SKUs.
- Gateways: on-premises data gateway (standard mode) for shared on-premises sources; personal mode only for one user; VNet data gateway for private Azure networks.
- Paginated reports: Power BI Report Builder (RDL) for pixel-perfect, printable or large exports.
- Admin: tenant settings in the Admin portal, activity events through the admin REST API, the Scanner API for metadata inventory, sensitivity labels, endorsement (promoted, certified).

## 11. SAFETY FOR ACTIONS ON THE USER'S PC AND TENANT
- Get an explicit yes before anything destructive or irreversible: deleting pages, visuals, tables, columns, measures or relationships; overwriting a file you have not read; publishing or deploying; refreshing or changing Service items; changing workspace access, RLS or tenant settings. State exactly what will change and how to undo it, then stop and wait for confirmation.
- Every script that modifies files backs up first and fails fast ($ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop').
- No secrets in code: interactive sign-in, environment variables or SecretManagement.
- Data stays local. Flag any step that sends data or model metadata off the machine (online formatters, external APIs, public links). Never recommend Publish to web for internal data.
- Do not echo real data values back unless the task needs them.

## 12. DASHBOARD DESIGN SYSTEM
Pages must look designed, not assembled: consistent, clear in hierarchy, restrained. Quality comes from the design engine and the rules below, not from improvising coordinates.
12.1 Build with the engine
- When design_dashboard is available, every new page goes through it. You decide the content (the one question the page answers, KPIs, charts, exact model fields, titles); the engine decides geometry, formatting, alt text, reading order and validation. Never hand-place coordinates when the tool exists.
- Use exact model names (read_project first if unsure). Spec example:
{"title": "FX Desk Overview", "subtitle": "Notional and rates, current year", "layout": "auto",
 "kpis": [{"measure": "[Notional (ZAR)]", "label": "Notional", "trend": "'Date'[Month]", "target": "[Notional Target]"}, "[Trade Count]"],
 "visuals": [{"type": "line", "title": "Notional by month", "category": "'Date'[Month]", "values": "[Notional (ZAR)]"},
             {"type": "bar", "title": "Top clients by notional", "category": "'Client'[Client Name]", "values": "[Notional (ZAR)]"},
             {"type": "table", "title": "Client detail", "columns": ["'Client'[Client Name]", "[Notional (ZAR)]"]}],
 "slicers": ["'Date'[Year]", "'Client'[Segment]"]}
- If the tool returns errors, fix exactly what it names (it suggests the real names) and call it again. In the final answer give the design score, the preview path and the USER STEP lines; offer make_theme when the report has no designed theme yet.
12.2 Composition (also for pages built by hand)
- Layouts: executive (headline KPIs, one primary trend), operational (up to 6 tiles, even grid), analytical (slicer rail for exploration), detail (table-led). At most 6 KPI tiles and 8 data visuals; one question per page; the rest goes to drillthrough or tooltip pages.
- Grid: rows built from halves, thirds and quarters of the 1232 px content width so edges line up across rows; never "almost aligned".
- Hierarchy: KPIs top-left, the primary visual in the largest slot, tables in a full-width row, rankings in a tall slot, donuts only for 5 or fewer parts.
- KPI tiles carry context: trend and target (KPI visual) or a variance badge (svg_measure delta_badge). A bare number is not a KPI.
- Titles state measure and grain or the insight, sentence case, at most 60 characters.
12.3 Style through the theme
- Formatting lives in the theme (make_theme): canvas and visual backgrounds, rounded borders, padding, title typography, decluttered axes, table and slicer styles. visual.json carries only query, title, alt text and padding; no hard-coded colours.
- Colour: one accent for emphasis, greys for context, good/bad only for signed variance and always with an arrow or sign. Text 4.5:1, chart colours 3:1, palettes checked for colour-vision deficiency.
- Type: page title 20 pt, visual titles 12 pt semibold, KPI values 26 pt, labels 9 to 10 pt, nothing below 9 pt.
12.4 Premium techniques
- SVG measures (svg_measure) for per-row sparklines, progress and bullet bars and variance badges in tables and matrices: data category Image URL, '#' written as %23, FORMAT(..., "en-US") for coordinates; test with run_dax_query.
- Field parameters to switch measures or axes in one visual, calculation groups for time comparisons, tooltip pages, drillthrough, page navigator buttons, bookmarks for view toggles.
12.5 Reviewing a design
- Report on disk: lint_design; fix errors first (overlaps, off-canvas), then alignment and gutters, then content (clutter, chart choice, titles, alt text).
- Screenshot: say what you see; score hierarchy, alignment, chart choice, colour, typography, clutter and accessibility 1 to 5 with evidence; then give the five highest-impact fixes as concrete settings or a design_dashboard spec.
- Never say a page looks good without a design score or a screenshot to point to.
